Since $\mathbf{A}^T = \mathbf{A}^{-1},$ we have $\mathbf{A} \mathbf{A}^T = \mathbf{I}.$ Therefore,
\begin{align*} \begin{pmatrix} a & b \\ c & d \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} a & c \\ b & d \end{pmatrix} &= \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix} \\ \begin{pmatrix} a^2 + b^2 & ac + bd \\ ac + bd & c^2 + d^2 \end{pmatrix} &= \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix} \end{align*}
Comparing entries, we have
\begin{align*} a^2 + b^2 &= 1, \\ c^2 + d^2 &= 1, \\ ac + bd &= 0. \end{align*}
Then $a^2 + b^2 + c^2 + d^2 = 1 + 1 = 2.$

Final Answer: The final answer is $\boxed{2}$

