Let $a = \sqrt{x^2 + y^2}, b = \sqrt{x^2 + z^2}, c = \sqrt{y^2 + z^2}.$ We want to show that
\[ \frac{ab + bc + ca}{abc} \geq 3 \cdot \sqrt{2}. \]
By AM-GM,
\begin{align*}
ab + bc + ca & = (a+b+c) \left( \frac{1}{4} a^2 + \frac{1}{4} b^2 + \frac{1}{4} c^2 - ab - ac - bc \right) \\
& \geq 3 \cdot \sqrt{2} \cdot \frac{\max(a,b,c)}{2} \\
& = 3 \sqrt{2}.
\end{align*}
Equality occurs when $a = b = c,$ so the minimum value is $\boxed{3 \sqrt{2}}.$
